{-
   Where a qualified value may **go** - the third of the conditions, and the only one that is about a
   use rather than a declaration.

   # resolver: ownership

   `Maybe('v)` **is** the type `Maybe(v)` - §2.4's whole design, and what makes `find` hand back an
   optional borrow without a view type - so nothing in the logical type distinguishes the two. What
   does distinguish them is the bytes: `membersOf` lays a record out through `fieldContract`, so a
   position the shape names is a reference where the declaration wrote a member. One logical type,
   two layouts, and a position that disagrees about which is which reads a pointer as the value it
   points at.

   `checkCarriedOrigins` is the one rule, asked wherever a value fills a declared position, and it is
   asked at **every convention** - which is what separates it from the extent half beside it in the
   same function. A borrow is bounded by the call it was granted for, so a callback holding this
   frame open for the duration is the feature rather than a mistake; a layout is not bounded by
   anything, and neither is what the callee's type says it may do with the value whole.

   In a file of its own for the reason `Reject.Residue.yana` is: this is checked while bodies
   resolve, and a program that fails a declaration check never gets a body.
-}

data Guard {id: I32}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {} = {}

data Cell(v) {value: v}

{-
   The instance the library brought here, and the reason this check exists rather than the
   declaration-time one that used to stand in for it.

   `copy` of a `Cell('v)` selects `Copy(v)` and duplicates what the borrow points at, because a
   borrow handed to a bare type variable binds what it points at - §2.4 again, read at the one place
   it costs something. `residualObligation` used to count the mere existence of this instance as a
   reason to refuse `cellOf`'s signature, which cost `Map.find`, `Map.findMut` and `Map.pairs` for a
   call none of them makes. The obligation is real and it belongs at the call.
-}
instance (Copy(v)) Copy(Cell(v)):
    fn copy(from: Cell(v)) -> Cell(v) = Cell {value: copy(from.value)}

fn cellOf(g: a'Guard) -> Cell(a'Guard) = Cell {value: g}

-- A **borrowed** parameter at the unqualified type, which is the case the extent half lets through:
-- it owns nothing and outlives nothing, and it still reads `value` as a `Guard` where the value has
-- an address. Before this rule it compiled and answered zero.
fn plain(c: Cell(Guard)) -> I32 = c.value.id

fn borrowed(g: a'Guard) -> I32 = plain(cellOf(g))

-- The same sentence at a **generic** parameter, which is what `copy` is. Nothing about `from: a`
-- says a layout, so there is nothing for the shape to agree with.
fn viaCopy(g: a'Guard) -> I32 = copy(cellOf(g)).value.id

-- And the other direction. A construction takes its shape from the destination, so the value that
-- arrives here unqualified is one that was already built - a local, whose own shape is its own.
fn wants(c: Cell(a'Guard)) -> I32 = c.value.id

fn member(->g: Guard) -> I32:
    let c = Cell {value: g}
    return wants(c)

fn main() -> I32 = 0
